    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="15" relname="span">Usability has to do with making technology easy to learn and easy to use.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="16" relname="span">I think the term is used to describe the quality of a user's experience when interacting with a computer,</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="2" relname="elaboration">whether its software, a cell phone, a website, or any other human operated device.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="17" relname="span">A usable system is one which enables users to perform their job effectively and efficiently (Pure Interface, 2005).</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="4" relname="elaboration">One way that this can be accomplished is through user-centered design.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="7" relname="preparation">In terms of something having ease of use,</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="18" relname="span">I think it has a lot to do with the users ability to effectively interact with technology, software, or other systems in a way that doesnt require a great deal of difficulty.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="13" relname="span">IBM has a website that is dedicated to nothing but the concept of ease of use, which I found very informative.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="8" relname="elaboration">IBM believes that ease of use is vital to the success of most products and services.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="13" relname="elaboration">They further point out that the user experience directly affects sales, service cost, productive use, customer loyalty and almost every other aspect of doing business.</segment>
        <group id="11" type="span"/>
        <group id="12" type="span" parent="11" relname="span"/>
        <group id="13" type="span" parent="19" relname="span"/>
        <group id="14" type="span" parent="12" relname="span"/>
